    "intro": {
      "type": "portrait-intro",
      "settings": {
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/portrait-artist.jpg",
        "heading": "My story",
        "lead": "<p>I'm Andrea Bowman. I paint in Cape Town, and I make things with my hands: large, layered canvases, and pots big enough to outlive me.</p>",
        "text": "<p>I trained at the Metafora Art School in Barcelona, but I learn just as much from wandering. I travel with a camera, and places stay with me long after I leave: the colours of a market, the heat of an Omani afternoon, a wall worn soft by weather. Sooner or later they find their way into paint, or into concrete. It still thrills me to see my paintings on the pages of five magazines, in rooms by designers I admire.</p>"
      }
    },
    "how_i_work": {
      "type": "narrow-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>When a painting takes hold, everything else falls away. The studio fills with paint and canvas and half-finished things, the days run together, and only the moment counts. I go somewhere outside my usual self, and I don't come back until the piece is right.</p><p>I work in layers of acrylic, oil, charcoal and mixed media, building up and scraping back, so earlier layers glow through the later ones. Every painting carries the history of the days it took to make.</p>"
          }
        },
        "quote_1": {
          "type": "quote",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "I love what I make, then I can't live with it, and a month later I love it all over again."
          }
        },
        "text_2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>There are days I wonder if I'm only posturing, and days when it simply flows and I know it. Both keep me honest, and both keep me painting. I am ambitious about my work, and I hope I never stop growing.</p><p>Then the storm settles into careful work: framing, finishing, the small last touches that let a painting leave the studio and go home.</p><p>The same hunger drives my pots. I came back from Oman with Omani pots in my head and had to make my own. Each one took five men to lift into place. They are nearly as large as I am, and they will be standing long after I'm gone.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
